peer_feedback_assignment_1:
  issue_number: 92
  issue_owner: jennywc
  issue_ok: yes
  issue_status: OK
  overall_comments:
    positive: One notable strength of this submission is how easy it is to follow.
      The report has a clear structure, and every table is followed by a little  explanation,
      so I always knew what you did and why you did it. The writing is simple and
      direct, and the key findings stand out clearly.
    negative: One area where the submission could be improved is interpreting the
      surprising patterns in the results instead of just reporting the numbers. For
      example, the 2023H1 catalog has 18,126 titles, much more than the 6,500 or so
      in later periods, but the report does not explain why that spike is there. The
      shows and movies table also has a large NA group in 2023H1, but the findings
      do not say what that means for the results. Explaining these patterns would
      make the analysis stronger and show the reader that you questioned your own
      results.
    advice: Two concrete steps would improve the submission. First, give the titles
      with missing runtime a label like "Unknown" instead of leaving them in an NA
      row, and add a short note explaining the 2023H1 catalog spike. Second, fix the
      "Initital Data Exploration" typo and vary the sentence openings that repeat
      "I grouped…" and "I believe…".
  scores:
    Written Communication:
      score: 8.0
      comment: 'The report is easy to follow and the structure is clear. You explain
        every table in simple words, so I know what you did and the reason behind
        the work. The memo is good because it connects the findings to the recommendations.
        Two small suggestions: first, many sentences start with "I grouped…" or "I
        believe…", you can change some of them to make it less repetitive. Second,
        the early observations look like notes, you can write them into full sentences.
        Also, for the memo, you can push one recommendation further. For example,
        based on the global content result, say what Netflix should do differently
        in 2027, not just keep investing.'
    Project Skeleton:
      score: 9.0
      comment: 'The project has a complete structure. All the sections are there:
        data acquisition, exploration, questions, table answers, memo, and the AI
        statement, and all the answers are in the right order. The document also renders
        well. One small mistake: the section heading says "Initital Data Exploration",
        there is a typo there. Fix that and the skeleton will be better.'
    Tables & Document Presentation:
      score: 7.0
      comment: 'The tables are clean and easy to read. The columns are labeled clearly,
        and the numbers and percents are formatted well. Things that can fix: when
        your code loads packages, R prints a lot of announcements, and those show
        up in the report between your code and your results. You can hide them so
        the report looks cleaner. Second, not every table has a title. Q1 and Q2 don''t
        have one, but Q3, Q4, and Q5 do. Adding titles to all of them would make it
        more consistent.'
    Data Visualization:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Exploratory Data Analysis:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Code Quality:
      score: 8.0
      comment: 'Your code is easy to follow, and your variable names make sense. The
        "Stranger Things" filter is careful too. Two things to improve: first, you
        load the same packages again in several places. You can load them once at
        the top, so the code is cleaner and you don''t get the same loading messages
        again and again. Second, some titles end up with no label: if a title has
        no "Season" in its name and its runtime is missing, your code can''t decide,
        so it falls into an NA row. In 2023H1 that row is 28% of viewing time. You
        can give those titles a label like "Unknown" so the table is complete.'
    Data Preparation:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Analysis and Findings:
      score: 8.0
      comment: 'The analysis answers all the questions, and the numbers are correct.
        The findings are clear, and the memo connects them to strategy well. Two findings
        could use more interpretation: first, the 2023H1 catalog has 18,126 titles,
        much more than the 6,500 or so in later periods, but you don''t explain why
        that spike is there. Second, the shows and movies table has a large NA group
        in 2023H1, but the findings don''t say what that means for the results.'
    Extra Credit:
      score: 0.0
      comment: There is no work have been done for extra credit.
  mp_number: 1.0
peer_feedback_assignment_2:
  issue_number: 103
  issue_owner: wijdane123-code
  issue_ok: yes
  issue_status: OK
  overall_comments:
    positive: One notable strength of this submission is how well you interpret surprising
      patterns instead of just reporting the numbers. For the catalog, you notice
      that 2023H1 has far more titles but fewer total hours, suggest it may include
      more short titles, and warn that the first period may not be fully comparable
      with later ones. For live events, you compare the average and the median to
      show that typical viewing is similar even though the average is much higher
      for pre-taped content. That kind of interpretation shows real thinking about
      what the numbers mean.
    negative: One area where the submission could be improved is explaining your data
      choices and definitions. The code fills in missing runtimes and defaults titles
      with no runtime information to movies, but the report never says so, so a reader
      cannot tell how those choices affect the results. The global title share is
      also stated without saying it counts titles available globally in at least one
      period. A sentence explaining each choice would make the analysis more transparent
      and easier to trust.
    advice: 'Three concrete steps would improve the submission. First, add a short
      note explaining your data choices: that missing runtimes were filled with the
      title''s average runtime, that titles with no runtime information default to
      movies, and that the global title share counts titles available globally in
      at least one period. Second, add a memo recommendation based on the global availability
      finding, since globally available titles are a minority of titles but a majority
      of hours viewed. Third, name the comparison period explicitly instead of saying
      "the earlier period," so the reader knows you mean 2023H2.'
  scores:
    Written Communication:
      score: 8.0
      comment: 'Your report is easy to follow and well organized. The initial exploration
        is thorough, and every table is followed by a clear explanation, so I always
        knew what you did and why. The live events explanation is especially good,
        because you compare the average and the median to show why they tell different
        stories. Two small points: you report both 35% of records and 24.6% of titles
        as globally available without explaining that they count different things,
        so a reader may get confused. Also, the memo says "the earlier period" without
        naming it, so it is not clear you mean 2023H2.'
    Project Skeleton:
      score: 9.0
      comment: 'The project has a complete structure. All the sections are there:
        data acquisition, exploration, table answers, memo, and the AI statement,
        and all the answers are in the right order. The document also renders well,
        with a table of contents. One small point: the exploratory findings are given
        as a numbered list without a section heading, so labeling that section would
        make the structure even clearer.'
    Tables & Document Presentation:
      score: 9.0
      comment: 'Your tables are clean and easy to read. The columns are labeled clearly,
        the numbers and percents are formatted well, and every table has a title.
        The document also looks clean because there are no package loading messages
        showing. One small point: the code is folded away by default, so a reader
        has to click to see it. Showing the code by default would make the analysis
        more transparent.'
    Data Visualization:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Exploratory Data Analysis:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Code Quality:
      score: 8.0
      comment: 'Your code is easy to follow, and you use tidyverse pipelines well.
        Using inline code for the memo numbers is good, because the memo always matches
        the calculations. Three things to improve: first, the code fills in missing
        runtimes with the title''s average runtime, but the report never says that,
        so a reader may not know the data was changed. Second, titles with no runtime
        information default to movies, but that choice is not explained either. Third,
        the show and movie check looks for "Season" with an exact capital S, so it
        could miss titles written with a small s. Explaining the first two choices
        in a sentence each would make the code more transparent.'
    Data Preparation:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Analysis and Findings:
      score: 8.0
      comment: 'Your analysis answers all the questions, and the numbers are correct.
        The findings are clear, and you explain the surprising patterns well, especially
        the 2023H1 catalog spike and the live events average compared with the median.
        One gap: the memo does not address the global availability finding, even though
        globally available titles are a minority of titles but a majority of hours
        viewed. That finding deserves a recommendation too. Also, the findings note
        that missing runtime may cause misclassification, but they do not spell out
        that the high 2023H1 movie share comes from titles with missing runtime defaulting
        to movies.'
    Extra Credit:
      score: 0.0
      comment: There is no work have been done for extra credit.
  mp_number: 1.0
peer_feedback_assignment_3:
  issue_number: 112
  issue_owner: andrewwilber
  issue_ok: yes
  issue_status: OK
  overall_comments:
    positive: 'One notable strength of this submission is that it always explains
      what the numbers mean. Every table starts with a Key Takeaway, so I knew what
      to look for before reading the numbers. The memo is also strong because it argues
      from specific evidence: it repeats the consistent titles table, cites exact
      shares for series and global content, and includes two charts that make the
      global and content-mix findings easy to see. That combination of takeaways,
      evidence, and visuals makes the argument easy to follow and hard to miss.'
    negative: One area where the submission could be improved is checking the details
      before submitting. The period end date for the first half is July 30 instead
      of June 30, the Stranger Things total differs from the stricter filter, and
      one table column carries the wrong header. None of these change the main story,
      but together they make the reader doubt the smaller details. A careful final
      check of dates, filters, labels, and number formats would catch all of them.
    advice: Three concrete steps would improve the submission. First, change the period
      end month for the first half from 7 to 6, so the date range ends June 30, 2026
      instead of July 30. Second, check the Stranger Things filter, using case-insensitive
      exclusions, to find why the total is 40,400,000 higher than expected. Third,
      fix the Table 1 share column header to "Share of Total Viewership" and format
      the top 10 share as a percent, 6.7%, instead of a raw proportion.
  scores:
    Written Communication:
      score: 7.0
      comment: 'our report is well organized, and the Key Takeaway before each table
        is a good idea, because I always knew what to look for in the table. The memo
        is also strong, because it argues from specific numbers instead of general
        claims. Three details need fixing: first, the date range says July 30, 2026,
        but the first half of a year ends June 30, so that date is wrong. Second,
        the top 10 share is given as a raw proportion, 0.066967, instead of a percent
        like 6.7%, which is hard to read. Third, the catalog takeaway says titles
        and runtime move in lockstep, but from 2023H1 to 2023H2 the title count drops
        sharply while runtime rises, so that is not accurate.'
    Project Skeleton:
      score: 9.0
      comment: 'The project has a complete structure. All the sections are there:
        data acquisition, exploration, table answers, memo, and the AI statement,
        and all the answers are in the right order. The Key Takeaway before each table
        also makes the structure easy to follow. One small point: the headings "Exploratory
        Questions-Inline Values" and "Exploratory Questions-Table Answers" are a bit
        confusing, because the tables are answers, not questions. Clearer headings
        would make the structure even better.'
    Tables & Document Presentation:
      score: 7.0
      comment: 'Your tables are clean and easy to read. The columns are labeled clearly,
        the numbers and percents are formatted well, and every table has a title.
        Thing that neeed to fix, the last column of Table 1 is headed "Table 1: Share
        of Total Viewership" instead of just "Share of Total Viewership," which looks
        like a labeling mistake.'
    Data Visualization:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Exploratory Data Analysis:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Code Quality:
      score: 7.0
      comment: 'Your code is easy to follow, and you use tidyverse pipelines well.
        The Key Takeaway structure and the inline values for the exploratory questions
        are good practice, because the numbers in the text always match the calculations.
        Three things need fixing: first, the period end date for the first half of
        each year is set to July 30 instead of June 30, so every first-half period
        has the wrong end date. Second, your Stranger Things total is 5,588,900,000,
        which is higher than the 5,548,500,000 from the stricter filter, most likely
        because your title match is broader and your exclusions are case-sensitive.
        Third, the last column of Table 1 is labeled "Table 1: Share of Total Viewership"
        instead of "Share of Total Viewership." Fixing these three details would make
        the code more reliable.'
    Data Preparation:
      score: 8.0
      comment: 'The period end date for the first half of each year is July 30 instead
        of June 30. If it then asks for a score, enter 8. If it asks why, paste this:
        The data preparation is thorough: dates, runtimes, titles, and live events
        are all cleaned, and missing runtimes are filled sensibly. But the period
        end date for the first half of each year is set to July 30 instead of June
        30, so every first-half period carries the wrong end date. Fixing that month
        would make the preparation correct.'
    Analysis and Findings:
      score: 7.0
      comment: 'Your analysis answers all the questions, and the memo is strong because
        it argues from specific numbers and includes evidence, like the consistent
        titles table and the charts. Three findings need attention: first, the date
        range ends July 30, 2026, but the first half ends June 30, so that finding
        is wrong. Second, the catalog takeaway says titles and runtime move in lockstep,
        but from 2023H1 to 2023H2 the title count drops sharply while runtime rises,
        so that takeaway does not fit the first period. Third, the shows and movies
        table has a large Unclassified group in 2023H1, 31.9%, but the findings do
        not explain what those titles are. Fixing these would make the analysis more
        reliable.'
    Extra Credit:
      score: 2.0
      comment: There is two well-formatted charted with the memo but no Gini Analysis,
        so only 2 points for extra credits.
  mp_number: 1.0
peer_feedback_assignment_4:
  issue_number: 118
  issue_owner: liuzhenfang689-sys
  issue_ok: yes
  issue_status: OK
  overall_comments:
    positive: 'One notable strength of this submission is that it is careful about
      what the data cannot show. Missing-runtime titles get an explicit "Unknown"
      label instead of being hidden in a blank group, so the reader can see exactly
      how much data is unclassified. The memo also raises a good limitation: higher
      watch time does not mean higher profit, because production costs matter too.
      That honesty about limits makes the analysis easier to trust.'
    negative: 'One area where the submission could be improved is explaining what
      the tables mean. The report gives correct tables but almost no interpretation:
      there is no paragraph after any table, so patterns like the 2023H1 catalog spike
      and the 28.0% Unknown group pass without comment. The memo is also narrow and
      leaves out the global availability finding entirely. Adding a short interpretation
      after each table and widening the memo would turn a correct report into a convincing
      one.'
    advice: Three concrete steps would improve the submission. First, add a short
      interpretation paragraph after each table, especially explaining the 2023H1
      catalog spike and the 28.0% Unknown group in 2023H1. Second, fix the Stranger
      Things filter by excluding Tales titles, so the total reflects only the five
      seasons. Third, add a memo recommendation based on the global availability finding,
      since globally available titles are a minority of titles but a majority of hours
      viewed.
  scores:
    Written Communication:
      score: 6.0
      comment: 'Your report is clear and simple, but it is quite thin on explanation.
        The initial exploration only talks about missing values, and there is no interpretation
        paragraph after any of the tables, so I often did not know what you thought
        the numbers meant. The memo is also short, and it has a typo, "wattime" instead
        of "watch time." Two small formatting points: the date range is given as raw
        dates, 2023-01-01 to 2026-06-30, instead of written-out months, and the title
        count is written as 24427 without a comma. Adding a short explanation after
        each table would improve the writing a lot.'
    Project Skeleton:
      score: 9.0
      comment: 'The project has a complete structure. All the sections are there:
        data acquisition and cleaning, initial exploration, exploratory questions,
        table answers, memo, and the AI statement, and all the answers are in the
        right order. The task-based headings make it easy to follow what each part
        is. One small point: both the inline values and the table answers restart
        numbering at Question 1, so it is a bit confusing which set a question belongs
        to.'
    Tables & Document Presentation:
      score: 7.0
      comment: Your tables are clean and easy to read. The columns are labeled clearly,
        the numbers and percents are formatted well, and every table has a title.
        Thing need to fix:the catalog table reports total runtime in minutes instead
        of hours, which makes it hard to compare with the usual hours figures. Reporting
        it in hours would be clearer.
    Data Visualization:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Exploratory Data Analysis:
      score: 7.0
      comment: The initial exploration uses summary instead of skim, and the only
        paragraph discusses missing values. It does not discuss the shape of the viewing
        hours, like the difference between the average and the median, or other patterns
        in the data. A fuller exploration with skim and a discussion of those patterns
        would make this section stronger.
    Code Quality:
      score: 7.0
      comment: 'Your code is easy to follow, and you use tidyverse pipelines well.
        Giving missing-runtime titles an explicit "Unknown" label is good, because
        it does not hide them in a blank group. Two things to fix: first, your Stranger
        Things filter excludes Beyond and Fireplace titles but not Tales titles, so
        the total of 5,588,900,000 includes companion titles and is not just the five
        seasons. Second, the memo shares are calculated but not used in the memo text,
        so that code does not do anything. Removing it or using it for the memo numbers
        would make the code cleaner.'
    Data Preparation:
      score: 10.0
      comment: DEFAULT SCORE
    Analysis and Findings:
      score: 6.0
      comment: 'Your analysis answers all the questions, and the numbers are mostly
        correct. But the report presents tables without explaining what they mean.
        There is no interpretation after any table, so patterns like the 2023H1 catalog
        spike and the 28.0% Unknown group in 2023H1 go unremarked. The memo is also
        narrow: it does not address the global availability finding, even though globally
        available titles are a minority of titles but a majority of hours viewed.
        Adding interpretations and a wider memo would make the analysis much stronger.'
    Extra Credit:
      score: 0.0
      comment: There is no work have been done for extra credit.
  mp_number: 1.0
evaluator: meng2026bc

